WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.543
Sustained wind is a more continuous flow, while gusts are shorter increases or shifts.

00:00:06.543 --> 00:00:16.042
Gust spread matters because an aircraft may appear stable between gusts yet briefly lose position, altitude, image stability, or control margin.

00:00:16.042 --> 00:00:20.475
Plan outbound and return legs with direction in mind.

00:00:20.475 --> 00:00:27.018
A tailwind outbound may become a headwind home, reducing groundspeed and increasing battery demand.

00:00:27.018 --> 00:00:31.873
Reserve energy for changing direction, route adjustment, approach, and landing.

00:00:31.873 --> 00:00:39.260
Airflow near buildings, trees, ridges, coastlines, cliffs, and terrain can accelerate, roll, descend, or become turbulent.

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:44.115
Open fields can also experience strong gusts and thermal movement.

00:00:44.115 --> 00:00:47.281
Conditions vary with altitude and position.

00:00:47.281 --> 00:00:49.603
Watch actual aircraft behavior.

00:00:49.603 --> 00:01:00.368
Increased tilt, poor position hold, low groundspeed, rising power use, vibration, or unstable footage can reveal conditions more severe than the launch area suggested.

00:01:00.368 --> 00:01:04.379
Do not normalize repeated warnings or control corrections.

00:01:04.379 --> 00:01:09.233
Payload size and shape can increase drag and change response.

00:01:09.233 --> 00:01:12.822
Manufacturer wind figures are not mission targets.

00:01:12.822 --> 00:01:19.365
Darkness, obstacles, narrow routes, inexperienced crew, or limited landing options justify lower operational thresholds.

00:01:19.365 --> 00:01:24.642
Crosswind during approach can also complicate landing near obstacles or people.

00:01:24.642 --> 00:01:32.874
Plan an approach direction with room to stabilize and go around or divert before battery pressure becomes urgent.

00:01:32.874 --> 00:01:39.840
Cancel or delay when gusts or turbulence threaten stable control, safe return, or usable data.

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:46.805
If conditions worsen in flight, turn back early while battery and landing choices remain generous.
